NNAAMMEE
     ls - list directory contents

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llss [-aaccddffggiillrrssttuu] [_f_i_l_e ...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     llss prints information about each named _f_i_l_e. By default, llss
     sorts by file name and prints only the name of each _f_i_l_e. If
     a _f_i_l_e is a directory, llss sorts and lists its contents (not
     including .  and .., unless -aa is given) rather than the
     directory's own name; the argument list itself is always
     sorted and shown in full first, and each directory's con-
     tents follow, headed by the directory's name if more than
     one argument was given.  If no _f_i_l_e is named, the current
     directory is listed.

     -aa   List all entries, including .  and ...

     -cc   Show (and, with -tt, sort by) the time the file's at-
          tributes were last changed (its ctime), instead of the
          modification time.

     -dd   Treat directories as plain files (list their own entry,
          not their contents).

     -ff   Force each argument to be treated as a directory
          (implies -aa, and turns off the ll, rr, ss, and tt options
          and all sorting).

     -gg   In the long format, print the group owner instead of
          the file's owner.

     -ii   Print the i-number of each file.

     -ll   Long format: mode bits, link count, owner (or group,
          with -gg), size in bytes (or major/minor device numbers
          for special files), time, and name.

     -rr   Reverse the sense of the sort.

     -ss   Print the size in blocks of each file.

     -tt   Sort by time, newest first, instead of by name.

     -uu   Sort by, and with -ll print, the access time instead of
          the modification time.

     When more than one of -cc,  -uu, and the default apply, the
     most recently given takes effect (they set the same internal
     selector).

     The mode field printed by -ll begins with one of the
     following:



